<p class="firstparagraph">Stephanie McMahon is one of the greatest Super Heavyweight fighters in the league.  One of the founding member of <i>Rampage Sports</i>, she was the reigning divisional champion when she joined us.  And that's how it was for a long time.  Either she had the title, or she was challenging to get it back.  But then it all started to go wrong.  One injury after another took her out of action, costing her valuable ranking points.  Slowly, she started to lose touch with the fighters at the top of the rankings.  Make no mistake... when she's right, Steph is still a threat to win against anyone.  But she just can't seem to stay healthy long enough to make another run at the belt.</p>

<p>Though her struggles have been a source of endless frustration, no one here is giving up on Steph.  We know she still has it, and we'll do whatever we can to push her back to the top.  In the meantime, Steph's lack of hardware is no concern... because the truth is she gets some of the credit for every trophy in the RSI case.</p>

<p>We didn't see it right away.  And, in what has become a common theme at RSI, there was a lot of drama before we figured it out.  But Steph has a gift most do not.  Though every woman in the RSI fold can fight on a level most only dream of, only a sparing few can use what they know to help others reach those levels, as well.  And none of them can do it the way Steph can.</p>

<p>Once we saw what Steph could do, we quickly realized we had something the other stables didn't.  Something so rare, we created a special position just to recognize it.  Fighter Development Coordinator.  A first-of-it's-kind position we created just for Steph.  Other groups have since followed suit, and I have to admit some of them have found very talented people to fill the role.  But ours is still the best... and that's not just talk.  We have the hardware to prove it.  In fact, the stable as a whole has attained a level of respect and prestige we never would have gotten close to without Steph.</p>

<p>So, what does a Development Coordinator do, you ask?  Well, since we made the role up, we got to make up the job description, too.  And in Steph's case, it just says <i>everything</i>.</p>

<p>Need to work on and improve your techniques?  Go see Steph.  Endurance and conditioning?  Steph handles that.  Whatever the question, Steph is the answer.  We don't ask her to do it alone, though.  Danni and I are heavily involved, and every weight class has at least one experienced and decorated athlete available to provide guidance and leadership.  But we're just the tools.  Steph is the brains behind it all.</p>

<p>What's Steph's most valuable asset?  Well, not to minimize everything else she does, but game-planning is where she really shines.  Not only does she know our own athletes, but she's intimately familiar with the skills and tendencies of their opponents, as well.  No RSI fighter goes into the octagon without a clear and thorough strategy crafted by Steph, and none would ever want to.  She may be gruff and almost rudely abrupt, but it never takes long for a fighter to realize how important Steph is.  Her demeanor is generally icy and serious because she has serious responsibilities.  To Steph, an unprepared fighter is a fighter in danger of serious injury... and we'll have none of that around here.  So, she may not be overly friendly, but the proof of how much she cares about her teammates is plain to see in the time and effort she puts into getting them ready.  Not only does she give them everything they need to feel confident when the cage door closes, but having that plan leaves them with the feeling that they're not alone in there.</p>

<p>What's Steph worth to us?  I think I speak for everyone at RSI when I say she's absolutely priceless.</p>